Additional member banks include institutions in [[Guajicutea]], [[Potocsí]], and [[Lacashe]] that joined the association after the founding. Member banks pay annual association dues proportional to their card issuance volume and share in the network's operating surplus through dividend distributions.

=== Card products ===

TpA licenses its brand and network to member banks, which issue TpA-branded cards to their customers. The company does not issue cards directly or extend credit to consumers; all card issuance, credit decisions, and customer relationships are managed by the member banks. TpA's card products include:

{{w|Credit card}}s are the network's primary product, issued by member banks under the TpA brand. Member banks set their own interest rates, credit limits, and fee structures, subject to TpA's network rules and local regulatory requirements. TpA-branded credit cards are accepted at all merchants equipped with TpA-compatible point-of-sale terminals across the six member markets.

{{w|Debit card}}s were introduced in 2004, linking directly to cardholders' bank accounts at member institutions. Debit cards have become the largest product category by transaction volume, driven by the growth of everyday electronic payments and the migration of salary and government benefit payments to electronic disbursement.

{{w|Prepaid card}}s were introduced in 2009, primarily targeting unbanked and underbanked segments of the population in markets where bank account penetration was lower. Prepaid TpA cards are sold through member bank branches and authorised retail agents, and are used for remittances, government benefit disbursements, and general-purpose stored-value payments.

== Ownership ==

TpA was originally structured as a member-owned cooperative association, with ownership shares allocated proportionally to member banks based on their card issuance volume. Following the 2007 IPO, the company transitioned to a publicly traded corporation listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker TPA. The five founding banks collectively retain approximately 76 per cent of the company's equity, with the remaining 14 per cent held by public investors and 10 per cent by other member banks that joined the association after the founding.

The company is governed by a board of directors consisting of representatives from the founding banks and independent directors elected by public shareholders. The board elects a chairman from among its members, and day-to-day operations are managed by a professional executive team led by a chief executive officer. Despite the public listing, TpA maintains its member-bank governance model in operational matters: member banks continue to participate in network rule-making, interchange fee setting, and strategic planning through the association's technical committees.

The company's hybrid structure means that operating surpluses are distributed both as dividends to public shareholders and as reduced interchange fees or reinvestment in network infrastructure for member banks. This structure was designed to balance the interests of external investors with those of the member banks that depend on the network for their payment card operations.

